                <pb facs="0027" n="21" xml:id="pg21"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Cablegram from Earl Li Hung Chang, dated the 7th
                        September, 1900, transmitted by the Chinese minister at St. Petersburg under
                        date of the 9th September and received by Minister Wu on the last-named
                        date.</hi></head>
                <dateline rendition="#center">[Handed to Acting Secretary Hill on <date
                        ana="#date_imprecise-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1900-09-10">September 10</date> by the Chinese minister, Mr. Wu
                    Ting-fang].</dateline>
                <p>I am in receipt of an imperial edict of the thirtieth day of the seventh month
                    (August 24, 1900) transmitted by way of Paoting Fu. It is as follows: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Li Hung Chang, envoy plenipotentiary, is hereby vested with full
                            discretionary powers, and he shall promptly deal with whatever questions
                            may require attention. From this distance we will not control his
                            actions. Let this edict be forwarded with extra expedition at the rate
                            of 600 li per day (to Earl Li) for his information and guidance. Respect
                            this.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
